body:
Generated MoE body kernels distribute activation work with nested expert/row loops and per-expert offset arithmetic. Use the router's existing `expert_major` vector to express the same worker assignment with one strided loop.

[#3895](https://github.com/positron-ai/tron/pull/3895) fixed helper imbalance by continuing row-to-helper assignment across expert boundaries: previously, every expert's first row went to helper 0. It implemented this global assignment with a running row count and modulo arithmetic inside the nested traversal. The router already exposed the desired row sequence in `expert_major` when that PR landed, so this change simplifies its implementation while retaining its balancing behavior.

With tracing, channel operations, and kernel arithmetic omitted, the generated traversal changes from:

```cpp
size_t n_prior_rows = 0;
for (const expert_id expert : routing->active_experts()) {
  const size_t n_rows = routing->per_expert[expert].size();
  const size_t first_row_ix =
    (actual_worker_ix + n_actual_workers - n_prior_rows % n_actual_workers)
    % n_actual_workers;
  for (size_t row_ix = first_row_ix; row_ix < n_rows; row_ix += n_actual_workers) {
    auto [token, slot] = routing->expert_row(expert, expert_row_id(row_ix));
    token_slot_id<4> ix(token, slot);
    // Process ix.
  }
  n_prior_rows += n_rows;
}
```

to:

```cpp
for (size_t row_ix = actual_worker_ix;
     row_ix < routing->expert_major.size();
     row_ix += n_actual_workers) {
  token_slot_id<4> ix = routing->expert_major[row_ix];
  // Process ix.
}
```

The constructor builds `expert_major` by concatenating the same experts' rows in the same order. Both loops assign global row `r` to helper `r % n_actual_workers`, preserving the existing balance across expert boundaries. Main-thread exclusion, single-worker behavior, trace placement, per-row channel waits/writes, and kernel arithmetic stay unchanged.

The accompanying comments clarify that expert-major order means grouping by ascending expert ID; matmul dispatch and completion can occur in a different order.

The existing MoE code-generation test checks the flat loop's worker offset, full row bound, stride, and token-slot lookup.
